{"key":"np","num":"89","title":"Nepal","blurb":"Every place GeoNames records above 500 people in Nepal: 84 towns and villages, grouped by region and ordered by population.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nepal","d":"Nepal is a landlocked country in South Asia. It is mainly situated in the Himalayas, but also includes parts of the Indo-Gangetic Plain. It borders the Tibet Autonomous Region of China to the north, and India to the south, east, and west, while it is narrowly separated from Bangladesh by the Siliguri Corridor, and from Bhutan by the Indian state of Sikkim. Nepal has a diverse geography, including fertile plains, subalpine forested hills, and eight of the world's ten highest mountains, including Mount Everest, the highest point above mean sea level on Earth. Kathmandu is the nation's capital and its largest city. Nepal is a multi-ethnic, multi-lingual, multi-religious, and multi-cultural sovereign state, with Nepali as the official language.\nParts of northern Nepal were intertwined with the culture of Tibet; that of the centrally located Kathmandu Valley is intertwined with the culture of Indo-Aryans, which was the seat of the prosperous Newar confederacy known as Nepal Mandala. The Himalayan branch of the ancient Silk Road was dominated by the valley's traders. The cosmopolitan region developed distinct traditional art and architecture. By the 18th century, the Gorkha Kingdom had unified Nepal under the Shah dynasty, which established the Kingdom of Nepal and later formed an alliance with the British Empire under the Rana dynasty of premiers. The country was never colonised but served as a buffer state between Imperial China and British India following the treaty of Sugauli. Parliamentary democracy was introduced in 1951 but was twice suspended by Nepalese monarchs, in 1960 and 2005. The Nepalese Civil War in the 1990s and early 2000s ended with the establishment of a secular republic in 2008, ending the world's last Hindu monarchy. The Constitution of Nepal, adopted in 2015, affirms the country as a federal parliamentary republic divided into seven provinces. \nNepal's foreign policy focuses on neutrality, sovereignty, and multilateral cooperation. Nepal was admitted to the United Nations in 1955, and friendship treaties were signed with India in 1950 and China in 1960. Nepal hosts the permanent secretariat of the South Asian Association for Regional Cooperation (SAARC), of which it is a founding member. Nepal is also a member of the Non-Aligned Movement and the Bay of Bengal Initiative. Nepal's foreign relations also emphasise development assistance, trade partnerships, and promoting peace and stability in South Asia.","groups":[{"name":"Bagmati Province","items":[{"n":"Kathmandu","u":"https://www.geonames.org/1283240","p":1442271,"t":"capital","w":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kathmandu"},{"n":"Bharatpur","u":"https://www.geonames.org/1283613","p":369377,"t":"city","w":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bharatpur,_Nepal"},{"n":"Pātan","u":"https://www.geonames.org/1282931","p":299283,"t":"city","w":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lalitpur,_Nepal"},{"n":"Hetauda","u":"https://www.geonames.org/1283339","p":195951,"t":"city","w":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hetauda"},{"n":"Madhyapur Thimi","u":"https://www.geonames.org/8411054","p":119955,"t":"city","w":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Madhyapur_Thimi"},{"n":"Banepā","u":"https://www.geonames.org/1283679","p":67629,"t":"town","w":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Banepa"},{"n":"Chautārā","u":"https://www.geonames.org/1283539","p":51347,"t":"town","w":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chautara"},{"n":"Panauti","u":"https://www.geonames.org/7963596","p":46595,"t":"town","w":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Panauti"},{"n":"Ki̇̄rtipur","u":"https://www.geonames.org/1283190","p":44632,"t":"town","w":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kirtipur"},{"n":"Chari̇̄koṭ","u":"https://www.geonames.org/1283546","p":40000,"t":"town","w":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bhimeshwar"},{"n":"Panaut